<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuno Álvares Pereira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1360">1360</a>–<a href="/years/1431">1431</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Saint Nuno of Saint Mary, Nuno Alvares Pereira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuno Álvares Pereira was a Portuguese general who served John I as constable of Portugal. During the crisis of 1383–1385 he defeated the Castilians at Atoleiros in 1384, Aljubarrota in 1385 and Valverde, securing Portuguese independence. He became one of the richest lords in the kingdom, then entered the Carmelite convent he had founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. He was canonised in 2009.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuno-alvares-pereira.jpg" alt="Nuno Álvares Pereira" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Condestavel-1554.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nuno Álvares Pereira was born on 24 June 1360 in Cernache do Bonjardim, the illegitimate son of Álvaro Gonçalves Pereira, prior of Crato, and Iria Gonçalves do Carvalhal. His grandfather Gonçalo Pereira was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> from 1326 until 1349. About a year after his birth he was legitimised by royal decree and so received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> education.</p><p>At 13 he became page to Queen Leonor and began military service in 1373, helping to stop an invasion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. At 16 he married Leonor de Alvim, a rich young widow. Of their three children, two sons died early, and their daughter Beatriz married Afonso, a son of King John I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When King Ferdinand I of Portugal died in 1383, his only heir was Beatrice, married to King John I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. To preserve independence, the nobles supported John, Master of Aviz. After his first victory over the Castilians at the Battle of Atoleiros in April 1384, John named Nuno protector and constable of Portugal, in practice supreme commander of the army, and count of Ourém. He was 24. He used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics against the Castilian army besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1384, until plague drove it away.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> recognised John as king in April 1385, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> invaded. On 14 August 1385 at Aljubarrota Nuno led 6,500 volunteers to victory over a Castilian force of more than 30,000. He attributed the victory to the Virgin Mary, whose name was inscribed on his sword. He was made count of Arraiolos and Barcelos and the realm's major majordomo, and the Portuguese defeated another larger Castilian army at Valverde. At his own expense he fed the hungry populations of his Castilian opposition, built churches and monasteries, among them the Carmelite church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and the church of Our Lady of Victories at Batalha, and when hostilities ended gave the bulk of his wealth to the veterans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his wife's death he joined the Carmelite order in 1423 at the Carmo Convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, which he had founded in fulfilment of a vow, and took the name Friar Nuno of Saint Mary. He lived there until his death on 1 November 1431, noted for prayer and penance and suffering from arthritis. In his last year King John I visited and embraced him, weeping, as his closest friend. His tomb was lost in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake">1755 Lisbon earthquake</a>.</p><p>He is known as the Saint Constable. Pope Benedict XV beatified him on 23 January 1918. In July 2008 Pope Benedict XVI promulgated the decrees recognising his heroic virtues and a miracle, and the canonisation was marked in Saint Peter's Square on 26 April 2009. His feast in Portugal is 6 November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuno Álvares Pereira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the crisis of 1383–1385 John, Master of Avis, and his constable <b>Nuno Álvares Pereira</b> defeated a much larger Castilian army at the Battle of Aljubarrota on 14 August 1385, with the help of English archers, and the Treaty of Windsor of 1386 began an alliance with England…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The army of John I of Portugal and his constable <b>Nuno Álvares Pereira</b>, with English allies, decisively defeated the larger army of John I of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-14">14 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cernache do Bonjardim, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Carmo Convent, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.82,-8.19&amp;year=1406" aria-label="Cernache do Bonjardim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nuno Álvares Pereira"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4464 4145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4464 4145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.82,-8.19&amp;year=1406">What was here in 1406? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
